1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,679
This month, Update examines breeder reactors, takes a look at CP&L's new environmental lab, and chats with employees at the tillery plant fish fry.

2
00:00:15,679 --> 00:00:26,019
Today, I'm Tom Hoyt.

3
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:27,420
And I'm Ray Gerstacker.

4
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:30,439
Welcome to Update for July 1982.

5
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,399
Last month's update featured a special look at some possible energy sources for the future.

6
00:00:36,179 --> 00:00:40,280
Today, more than ever before in the history of man, it's essential to plan ahead for energy.

7
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:44,359
During this past century, the worldwide industrial revolution

8
00:00:44,359 --> 00:00:51,120
and resulting changes in lifestyle gave rise to a relatively sudden upsurge in the need for energy.

9
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:55,420
But many conventional sources of energy are simply running out.

10
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:59,540
And yet, one natural resource can still be found in abundance.

11
00:01:00,359 --> 00:01:04,599
Scientists agree that the energy contained in uranium has not yet reached its full potential.

12
00:01:05,219 --> 00:01:08,200
And if certain groups in this country have their way, it never will.

13
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:15,159
What we're speaking of is the Breeder Reactor, one step beyond nuclear power as we know it today.

14
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:21,100
Recently, we visited Oak Ridge, Tennessee, and the Clinch River Breeder Reactor Project,

15
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:26,760
which is jointly sponsored by the U.S. government, private utilities, and manufacturers.

16
00:01:27,879 --> 00:01:34,460
But before we take a look at that project, Ray and I wanted to visit here at the North Carolina State University School of Nuclear Engineering

17
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:37,320
to find out what makes the Breeder Reactor so special.

18
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:41,880
Hi, Tom.

19
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:45,400
Glad to have you at North Carolina State University.

20
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:46,560
Well, thank you very much.

21
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:47,160
Glad to be here.

22
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:51,620
We're speaking with Dr. Robert Cockrell, director of the Nuclear Reactor Program.

23
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:58,140
Bob, how about telling us what a breeder reactor is and how it might differ from the reactors in use today?

24
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:01,620
Ray, a breeder reactor is a special kind of reactor.

25
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:06,240
While it generates electricity, it actually makes more fuel than it burns.

26
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:13,140
It differs somewhat from the kinds of reactors you have down in Robinson and Brunswick in essentially two ways.

27
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:19,200
One is in the kind of fuel it uses, and the other is the way it cools the core of the reactor.

28
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:20,659
Let me tell you about the fuel first.

29
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:26,680
Now, the reactors that you have at Brunswick and Robinson use a mixture of two kinds of uranium.

30
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:30,480
Most of the uranium in the reactor is non-fissionable.

31
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:32,540
We call that uranium U-238.

32
00:02:33,379 --> 00:02:35,780
The other part is fissionable uranium.

33
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:37,219
We call that U-235.

34
00:02:37,319 --> 00:02:44,939
Now a breeder reactor uses a mixture of plutonium and non-fusionable uranium.

35
00:02:44,939 --> 00:02:50,340
Now the interesting thing about that fuel is that the plutonium is

36
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:54,840
fissionable and as it's fissioning and generating heat and making electricity

37
00:02:54,840 --> 00:03:01,139
at the same time it turns that non-fusionable uranium into plutonium.

38
00:03:01,139 --> 00:03:05,280
It makes some more of itself. So what actually happens is that after you've

39
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:11,520
operated for say a year and you shut down and you take the fuel out of the reactor and you reprocess

40
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:18,240
it you find that you have more plutonium than you had at the beginning of the year now you can mix

41
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:23,759
that with some more of that non-fusionable uranium make more fuel stick it back into the reactor and

42
00:03:23,759 --> 00:03:29,360
you have a little bit of plutonium left over now you can save that and after about 10 years you've

43
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:33,920
got enough to make another reactor for heaven's sake now let me tell you about the the coolant

44
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:39,920
to that other point. Now your operators at the Robinson and Brunswick plant are used to

45
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:45,520
using water to cool the reactor and actually your Brunswick plant is called a boiling water reactor

46
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:53,760
and the Robinson plant is a pressurized water reactor. A breeder uses liquid metal or liquid

47
00:03:53,760 --> 00:04:01,120
sodium so we call the breeder a liquid metal breeder reactor. Is the breeder a brand new

48
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:04,680
technology Bob or have we already been experimenting with it here in this

49
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:09,159
country? Oh Ray, the breeder reactor is not a new technology. In fact I think the

50
00:04:09,159 --> 00:04:15,900
breeder reactor is an older technology than water reactors. In fact in 1951 the

51
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:20,860
very first nuclear reactor in the whole world to generate electricity was a

52
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:25,360
breeder reactor. Started up in Idaho in 1951 and then there have been several

53
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:31,840
breed of reactors that have been started up and run for a number of years there's

54
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:41,080
another one experiment of greater reactor number two started up in the 1960s most recently a very large test reactor called the fast flux test

55
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:47,639
facility started up in 1980 in the state of Washington now this reactor is used

56
00:04:47,639 --> 00:04:53,980
to test fuel for breeder reactors and materials but it does unfortunately does

57
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:59,819
not generate electricity. So we still need to build a plant that generates electricity.

58
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:04,259
Bob, since we already have a Hanford breeder reactor, why do we need to build another one

59
00:05:04,259 --> 00:05:10,700
at Clinch River? The Hanford breeder reactor is a test reactor. In fact, it's called a fast

60
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:16,360
flux test facility, and its purpose is to test fuel and materials for the breeder reactor.

61
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:23,200
Now, a test reactor is designed to be run by a research scientist. It has a lot of extra

62
00:05:23,199 --> 00:05:28,680
instrumentation in it. And you know also, when a scientist runs a reactor, he doesn't worry too

63
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:34,579
much if he's down for a few extra weeks while he sets up his experiment. Now, when you run a reactor

64
00:05:34,579 --> 00:05:40,079
to generate electricity for the customer, you're very conscious of downtime. You're also conscious

65
00:05:40,079 --> 00:05:45,120
of being able to operate and maintain that reactor with a normal everyday person, with a lot of

66
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:51,740
training, of course, like the people you have at Brunswick and Robinson. So what we need is a

67
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:57,860
reactor, a power plant, a breeder power plant that is designed for utility that

68
00:05:57,860 --> 00:06:01,360
generates electricity that can be run by everyday people.

69
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:07,000
Well Bob, how long has this Clinch River project been in the planning stage?

70
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,240
The planning for a breeder reactor to demonstrate the capability to generate

71
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:17,699
electricity started back in the 1960s. Now the Clinch River site was selected

72
00:06:17,699 --> 00:06:23,459
around oh late 1972 or 73 so the clench river breeder reactor project has been in existence

73
00:06:23,459 --> 00:06:30,219
about 10 years let's go now to oak ridge and project manager bill roth for a look at the

74
00:06:30,219 --> 00:06:37,419
clench river project today at what stage in the development or the construction of the project

75
00:06:37,419 --> 00:06:47,019
are you in now we're in the uh the period of heavy licensing activities uh where by and large about

76
00:06:47,019 --> 00:06:56,979
about 85% complete with the engineering, about 95% complete with the research associated

77
00:06:56,979 --> 00:07:03,740
with the project, and we're moving into the area where we want to start construction.

78
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:08,159
But as with any nuclear plant in order to start construction you have to have a license

79
00:07:08,159 --> 00:07:12,139
and that means we're interacting very heavily now with the Nuclear Regulatory Commission

80
00:07:12,139 --> 00:07:14,539
to give us the permission to start the construction.

81
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:18,939
I see. Now, what equipment do you have on site at this point?

82
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:21,080
We have a lot of equipment on site.

83
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:29,939
There's over a quarter of a billion dollars' worth of equipment that has been delivered

84
00:07:29,939 --> 00:07:38,720
or is in the testing phase, delivered to storage or now being tested in various parts of the country.

85
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:47,360
we've a lot of it is here in Oak Ridge roughly between 50 and 100 million dollars of equipment

86
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:55,040
is stored here in Oak Ridge and the rest is in various other locations around the country we

87
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:01,220
have about a half billion dollars of a little over a half billion dollars of equipment committed in

88
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:07,640
procurements for the fabrication of it delivery which is is the majority of the major plant

89
00:08:07,639 --> 00:08:13,599
components and so we're quite what well along with the the engineering

90
00:08:13,599 --> 00:08:20,479
fabrication and activities leading to the thing we'd like to do next and that

91
00:08:20,479 --> 00:08:24,819
start construction well do you have any projections as to when construction will

92
00:08:24,819 --> 00:08:33,000
be finished we hope to operate the plant before the the end of this decade before

93
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:41,159
before 1990. And we believe we can make it if we have an efficient licensing process

94
00:08:41,159 --> 00:08:45,740
that allows us to start construction very shortly. We've been ready to start construction

95
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:56,320
since 1977 with our plans and activities. And we were held up by administrative actions

96
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:02,860
in Washington, D.C. The Nuclear Regulatory Commission in 1977 was asked to suspend the

97
00:09:02,860 --> 00:09:07,500
public hearings then that day in that year would have led to the ability to

98
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:11,740
start construction the site so we've been ready since then I also asked mr.

99
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:16,659
Rolf how long the fuel that we now have sitting in storage yards would last if

100
00:09:16,659 --> 00:09:22,940
we committed to an active breeder program his answer was most surprising

101
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:31,899
at today's total electricity consumption rate this fuel resource that's already

102
00:09:31,899 --> 00:09:39,659
in mind and is in storage would last at least two centuries at today's consumption rates for

103
00:09:39,659 --> 00:09:45,340
total electricity so we actually have the fuel sitting around america today and our

104
00:09:46,059 --> 00:09:52,779
we also have the know-how and our choice is do we use stuff that we already have or do

105
00:09:52,779 --> 00:09:59,980
we go a much more expensive route with coal and oil is that our choice mr rolfe thank you very

106
00:09:59,980 --> 00:10:06,779
much for talking to us thank you bill ralph indicated that the clinch river breeder project

107
00:10:06,779 --> 00:10:11,580
has been delayed about five years are other countries experiencing the same kind of delay

108
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:16,539
no tom the other countries are not experiencing the same delay in fact the russians started up

109
00:10:16,539 --> 00:10:22,300
a demonstration breeder reactor in 1972 and then they started up a commercial breeder reactor of

110
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:29,899
600 megawatt electric size in 1976 so they've been operating that one for six years we also know that

111
00:10:29,899 --> 00:10:36,620
the United Kingdom, Germany, France and Japan have breeder reactors now operating.

112
00:10:36,620 --> 00:10:42,779
Congress still hasn't appropriated funds for the continuation of this project. Bob, if the

113
00:10:42,779 --> 00:10:47,179
okay were given tomorrow, how long do you think it would be before we'd have commercial reader

114
00:10:47,179 --> 00:10:52,620
reactors in this country? Ray, I have to make several assumptions. One assumption I'll make

115
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:57,579
is that we would like to first build the Clinch River breeder reactor so we can get some operating

116
00:10:57,580 --> 00:11:02,300
experience before we start constructing big commercial reactors so let's say we're going

117
00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:12,139
to do that well the breeder is now ready to begin construction and it's such a large plant and it's

118
00:11:12,139 --> 00:11:18,700
a new plant i would guess that probably if we started preparing the site tomorrow that it would

119
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:25,820
be at least eight years before the plant went into operation that carries us to 1990. and let's assume

120
00:11:25,820 --> 00:11:30,220
that along the way that we were beginning to design a large commercial breeder reactor

121
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:35,660
but we didn't want to finish the design until the clinch river reactor operates a little bit

122
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:43,500
so let's say that it would be all around 1992 or 93 before we would start building the big

123
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:49,020
breeder reactor well if it takes about eight or nine or ten years to build one of those reactors

124
00:11:49,020 --> 00:11:54,220
that will carry us beyond the year 2000. so i'm going to say that it will be about the year

125
00:11:54,220 --> 00:12:01,259
2005 before we could have large commercial breeder reactors in operation in the united states

126
00:12:02,139 --> 00:12:09,180
if we start right now with our program build that breeder now well that still puts us 20 years

127
00:12:09,180 --> 00:12:16,460
behind the rest of europe doesn't it well yes it does but i'm not nearly as concerned about being

128
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:21,899
behind the rest of europe as i am about another fact i heard the other day i heard the other day

129
00:12:21,899 --> 00:12:28,019
that we have enough fissionable uranium to last us about 30 or 40 more years.

130
00:12:28,840 --> 00:12:36,159
After that, uranium will become quite expensive as we have to go into the mine the ores that have less and less uranium.

131
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:39,639
That's going to affect the price of electricity, isn't it?

132
00:12:39,919 --> 00:12:43,120
Well, it sure is, Ray, because we know what happened to the price of electricity

133
00:12:43,120 --> 00:12:44,860
when the cost of oil zoomed up.

134
00:12:45,319 --> 00:12:49,500
And we can expect the same thing to happen when the cost of uranium goes up.

135
00:12:50,059 --> 00:12:51,980
It's sure going to affect our electric bills.

136
00:12:52,419 --> 00:12:54,240
I think we need to get started on that breeder.

137
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:57,179
Thank you very much, Bob, for talking to us.

138
00:12:57,240 --> 00:12:58,480
Ray, I enjoyed it. It was a pleasure.

139
00:12:59,659 --> 00:13:06,340
Although commercial breeder reactors are not yet a reality, there are other products of human ingenuity which can be put to use today.

140
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:10,399
Many of these innovations can be found at the World's Fair in Knoxville.

141
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:12,080
Barry Perry explains.
